    "ac40c473b45b8920": {
      "text": "CONTEXT: Two work paths P and H drain via N leaderless agents; split q = n_P/n_H is agent counts; backlogs B_P, B_H are counter-derived only (queued + unacked, decremented on commit, incremented on arrival). Goal implied by \"co-termination\": both paths finish at the same time, so no agent idles waiting on the other path. The split must be computed from measurements, never from a plan. ROLE: Control-loop designer for a self-allocating swarm; you produce one buildable mechanism, not a survey. TASK: Specify the mechanism that makes both backlogs hit zero simultaneously by driving the agent split from measured backlog ratios. CONSTRAINTS: One mechanism only. Every quantity must be measurable from the named counters or agent-observable events. No leader, no global clock assumption beyond periodic local reads. ANTI-GOALS: no planned/predicted workloads, no centralized scheduler, no optimization narrative, no padding. INPUTS: The SIGNAL table (B_P, B_H counter semantics; q definition; N sovereign agents). SUCCESS: A reader can implement the controller from the mechanism text alone, and the test would falsify it if the co-termination claim is false. OUTPUT: THE MECHANISM / WHY IT MATTERS / THE TEST, each concrete. **THE MECHANISM — Measured-Ratio Split Controller with Hysteresis** Each agent runs the same local loop, tick τ (or event-triggered on commit/arrival): **Read** B_P, B_H from the counters (never from a plan — Supported by the signal's measurement spec). **Estimate drain rates** μ_P, μ_H as exponentially-weighted means of per-agent commits/sec observed on each path (each agent measures its own path's commit rate; no global state needed — Unproven that local EWMA suffices under heterogeneity; the test covers this). **Compute target split**: q* = (B_P/μ_P) / (B_H/μ_H). This is the ratio that makes both paths' time-to-empty equal: T_P = B_P/(n_P·μ_P), T_H = B_H/(n_H·μ_H); setting T_P = T_H yields q*. (Supported: algebraic identity given the rate model; the rate model itself is the falsifiable part. **Migrate with hysteresis**: an agent switches paths only if |n_P/n_H − q*| exceeds a deadband δ (e. , δ = 1/N, i. , one agent's worth of error). This prevents thrash when backlogs are near equal or noisy. Switching is a local decision; two agents switching simultaneously is tolerated because the next tick re-measures and corrects (Supported: the loop is self-correcting by construction; worst case is bounded oscillation of ±1 agent — Unproven under adversarial arrival correlation). **Terminal behavior**: when either backlog hits zero, all agents flood to the remaining path. Co-termination error is then bounded by one tick τ plus migration latency. Degenerate cases handled by the same rule: B_P = 0 → q* → ∞ → all agents to H (consistent with flood-to-remaining). μ measured as zero on an untouched path → seed with one agent to get a rate sample before trusting q* (flagged: cold-start is the weakest link; Unproven without the test). **WHY IT MATTERS:** A static split guarantees idle agents on whichever path finishes first; tying q to the live measured ratio B/μ is the only leaderless way to make makespan = max-path work ÷ N instead of the sum of two sequential drains. **THE TEST:** Simulate (or run) N = 16 agents under bursty arrivals with a 3:1 backlog imbalance and a 2:1 rate asymmetry. Falsification criterion: with the controller, |T_P − T_H| / max(T_P, T_H) > 10% at co-termination, or makespan fails to beat the best static split by ≥ 20% — either outcome kills the mechanism. Control condition: the best fixed q found by grid search. If the controller cannot beat every fixed split under arrival jitter it cannot defend its measurement cost; if it can, the rate-model assumption (step 3) is Supported, not just Unproven.",
